ned \"Train 2\" and see that with no height 3 obstacles, the \"2\" reaches the end, which correlates to the appearance of a flag. I now need more data to validate it is a height 3 rule.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Trigger**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the rules governing where the \"2\" halts. My hypothesis is that the height of the preceding obstacle dictates the stopping point. I'm focusing on the condition to stop or not stop in the test data now. Specifically, I'm examining how column heights dictate where it stops. It looks like columns with height 3 stop the \"2\"s movement, which is shown in train 2. Then I noticed the appearance of a cross. Now, the key seems to be where these crosses appear relative to the 3s. I'm finding strong connections with where crosses appear in train 2 and train 4.\n\n\n**Formulating the Jumping Rules**\n\nI think I've cracked it! The \"2\" isn't just stopping at a random point. I've realized it's a representation of a Mario-style jump! I'm now testing the hypothesis that it can clear obstacles of height 2 or less. I'm seeing this confirmed with Train 1 and Train 3, and I'm looking at Train 2, which seems to confirm it's clearing all the low obstacles.\n\n\n**Confirming the Stop Criteria**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the behavior of Mario in the test input. It's stopping before the height-3 column, as expected. But, it stops on column 2, meaning it never clears the level. This confirms the impassable nature of a height-3 obstacle. I've re-examined the sky objects, and confirmed they are scenery only, with no effect on the Mario's trajectory.\n\n\n",
